  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://w3id.org/scholarlydata/inproceedings/iswc2016/paper/posterdemo/demo-61">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://purl.org/spar/fabio/ProceedingsPaper"/>
    <owl:sameAs rdf:resource="http://data.semanticweb.org/conference/iswc/2016/paper/posterdemo/demo-61"/>
    <j.3:keyword rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Data publication</j.3:keyword>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dul/DUL.owl#SocialObject"/>
    <j.13:maker rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/pierre-yves-vandenbussche"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/gofran-shukair"/>
    <j.3:abstract>Considerable investment in RDF publishing has recently led to the birth of the Web of Data. But is this investment worth it? Are publishers aware of how their linked datasets traffic looks like?
We propose an access analytics platform for linked datasets. The system mines traffic insights from the logs of registered RDF publishers and extracts Linked Data-specific metrics not available in traditional web analytics tools.
We present a demo instance showing one month (December 2014) of real traffic to the British National Bibliography RDF dataset.</j.3:abstract>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/neil-wilson"/>
    <dc:subject rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Linked Data</dc:subject>
    <j.3:title>Access Logs Don't Lie: Towards Traffic Analytics for Linked Data Publishers</j.3:title>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/luca-costabello"/>
    <j.3:keyword rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Access analytics</j.3:keyword>
    <j.3:keyword rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Linked Data</j.3:keyword>
    <j.13:maker rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/gofran-shukair"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/corine-deliot"/>
    <j.2:abstract>Considerable investment in RDF publishing has recently led to the birth of the Web of Data. But is this investment worth it? Are publishers aware of how their linked datasets traffic looks like?
We propose an access analytics platform for linked datasets. The system mines traffic insights from the logs of registered RDF publishers and extracts Linked Data-specific metrics not available in traditional web analytics tools.
We present a demo instance showing one month (December 2014) of real traffic to the British National Bibliography RDF dataset.</j.2:abstract>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/ontology/conference-ontology.owl#InProceedings"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dul/DUL.owl#Object"/>
    <j.3:hasAuthorList rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/scholarlydata/inproceedings/iswc2016/paper/posterdemo/demo-61/authorList"/>
    <j.13:maker rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/neil-wilson"/>
    <dc:creator rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/pierre-yves-vandenbussche"/>
    <dc:title>Access Logs Don't Lie: Towards Traffic Analytics for Linked Data Publishers</dc:title>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.ontologydesignpatterns.org/ont/dul/DUL.owl#InformationObject"/>
    <dc:subject rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Data publication</dc:subject>
    <j.13:maker rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/luca-costabello"/>
    <j.13:maker rdf:resource="http://www.scholarlydata.org/person/corine-deliot"/>
